    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter tonight\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling lineup led by **Christian Yelich** (74.33) and **Junior Caminero** (71.46). **Christian Yelich** earns the top spot on our board today, fueled by a stellar 19% boost from a favorable matchup against Feltner and an aggressive 13% platoon advantage. Similarly, **Junior Caminero** is positioned for a productive outing; despite a park factor penalty, his elite 0.376 xwOBA and a generous 11% skill-based boost against Bibee cement his status as a cornerstone start. We also see high value in **Colt Keith** (69.64) and **Yordan Alvarez** (66.89), both of whom benefit from optimal batting order placement and favorable platoon splits against Wacha and Burke, respectively. \n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Caleb Durbin**, whose 46.27 score landed him just outside our preferred starting nine. While **Caleb Durbin** carries the stolen base potential we covet, the model favored **Josh Smith** (46.92) due to the latter\u2019s massive 14% dynamic platoon advantage, which narrowly pushed him into the lineup to maximize our daily efficiency. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (59.13) represents a calculated risk today; despite an excellent 14% platoon edge, he is facing the elite arm of Skenes. We are counting on **Pete Crow-Armstrong**\u2019s speed to negate the 2% penalty incurred by Skenes' elite catch-and-throw defense, but he remains a high-variance play. \n\nManagers should keep a close eye on the weather and the final lineup cards, particularly for **Kazuma Okamoto** and **Trea Turner**. **Trea Turner** faces a daunting matchup against the talented Weathers, and his score is depressed by a 13% penalty against the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill level; however, his unique ability to contribute across all categories\u2014specifically his 20% weight in the stolen base sector\u2014makes him an essential, if risky, start. We have opted to keep **Lars Nootbaar**, **Carson Benge**, and **Pedro Ramirez** on the bench, as their individual projections failed to cross the 40.0 efficiency threshold required to displace our current starters.",
